setState() doesn't itself change the state. You change the variable/state inside setState(), and then setState() tells Flutter:

“The state has changed, rebuild this part of the UI.”

Example:

setState(() {
  count++;
});

Here:

count++ → changes the state
setState() → tells Flutter to rebuild the widget
Interview answer

“setState() is used in a StatefulWidget to notify Flutter that the state has changed, so Flutter rebuilds the widget with the updated state.”

Mental flow
State changes
     ↓
setState()
     ↓
Flutter schedules rebuild
     ↓
build() runs again
     ↓
Updated UI